 ## tab-keys.mjs
 
-### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others
-- Since: 2026-09-02
-- Decision: a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`).
-- Forbidden: renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous.
-- Reason: the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed one
-  and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the "close keeps keys" test).
+### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab renumbers the others (overridden 2026-10-23)
+- Since: 2026-10-23. The maintainer overrode the 2026-09-02 decision below. The anchor keeps its old name so existing
+  references still resolve.
+- Decision: closing a tab renumbers the remaining tabs in their current order so keys stay contiguous (`t1,t2,t3`,
+  close `t2` → `t1,t2`, where the new `t2` is the old `t3`). `next` is reset to one past the last tab, so the next
+  opened tab gets the next contiguous key (`t3`).
+- Rejected: the 2026-09-02 decision — a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted, and closing a
+  tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`). Its reason: the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab
+  to the right of the closed one and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced
+  by the former "close keeps keys" test).
+- Known cost: that reason still applies. A close now changes the React key and the URL entry of every tab after the
+  closed one. Consumers that key components or per-tab state on `key` will remount those tabs or attach state to the
+  wrong tab.
 - Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
